# Line-ending policy.
#
# Git already stores this repository's text as LF. Without this file a Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true produces a CRLF working tree, which then
# fails `mvn spotless:check` whenever the build runs on Linux against that tree
# (a Maven container, or a bind-mounted Docker build) — every file reports a
# violation whose entire diff is \r\n -> \n. CI on ubuntu-latest never saw it,
# because a fresh Linux checkout is already LF.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the working tree LF on every platform, matching both what
# Git stores and what google-java-format expects. gitattributes takes precedence
# over core.autocrlf, so no per-machine Git config is required.

* text=auto eol=lf

*.java       text eol=lf
*.xml        text eol=lf
*.yml        text eol=lf
*.yaml       text eol=lf
*.json       text eol=lf
*.sql        text eol=lf
*.md         text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
Dockerfile   text eol=lf
*.sh         text eol=lf
mvnw         text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF to remain executable by cmd.exe.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

*.jar   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
